course: everyday
lesson: 120
title: Home myths, first aid and weather warnings
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some popular beliefs about home safety are simply wrong, and believing them can hurt you: a switch can fail, germs do not wait five seconds, and an open door does not stop fire.
explain: A good habit is to ask «how would this really work?»: germs are on the floor already, so they get onto dropped food at once, and smoke goes wherever air goes, so only a closed door slows it down.
explain: Small injuries at home have simple first aid: press a clean cloth on a cut to stop the bleeding, and let a grown-up take out a splinter with clean tweezers.
explain: Big weather dangers come with warnings, and a family that follows its emergency plan and listens to official alerts stays safe.
explain: For example, in a flood a family in Louisiana goes upstairs instead of wading outside, because even shallow floodwater can be fast, dirty and electrified.
fact: A switch can fail, so an appliance is unplugged before anyone touches the inside.
fact: Cleaning products such as bleach must never be drunk, even a little, because they burn the throat.
fact: The five-second rule is not true: germs stick to dropped food at once, so if in doubt, throw it away.
fact: Only a closed door slows a fire, and fire doors are best; an open door lets smoke through.
fact: Pressing a clean cloth on a cut and raising the hand helps to stop the bleeding.
fact: A splinter should be removed by a grown-up with clean tweezers, because dirty splinters get infected.
fact: Icy steps make people fall, so walk carefully and tell a grown-up to grit or salt them, which means spreading tiny stones or salt so feet grip and the ice melts.
fact: During a hurricane, tornado or bushfire warning, the family follows its emergency plan and listens to official alerts.
fact: In a flood, move up to higher floors and never walk in floodwater, which can be fast, dirty and electrified.
exercise: Your friend says a toaster is safe to poke inside once it is switched off. What is true? | Unplug it first, because switches can fail | Switched off means completely safe | It is only safe if it is cold | It is safe if you use a metal knife and only poke in quickly
exercise: Someone says a tiny sip of bleach does no harm. What is the truth? | Never drink cleaning products, because they burn the throat | A small sip is fine as long as you drink lots of water straight after | Bleach is only harmful if it is hot | Bleach cleans you from the inside
exercise: You drop a biscuit on the kitchen floor and pick it up in three seconds. Is it free of germs? | No, germs stick to it at once | Yes, germs need five seconds to climb on | Yes, if the floor looks quite clean | Yes, if you blow on it hard first
exercise: Which door slows a fire best? | A closed fire door | An open fire door, wedged wide | Any door, open or closed | A door with a gap at the bottom
exercise: You cut your finger while helping to cook. What should you do? | Press a clean cloth on it, raise your hand and tell a grown-up | Let it bleed freely to clean it | Hold it under the hot tap | Wrap it in a paper napkin and keep cooking
exercise: You get a splinter in your hand. What is best? | Ask a grown-up to remove it with clean tweezers | Dig it out yourself with a pin you found on the floor | Leave it in for good | Bite it out
exercise: The front steps are covered in ice. What do you do? | Walk carefully and tell a grown-up so the steps can be gritted | Run down quickly before you slip | Slide down them for fun | Pour hot water on them to melt the ice
exercise: The radio gives a tornado warning for your town in Oklahoma. What should your family do? | Follow the family emergency plan and listen to official alerts | Go outside to watch the tornado | Ignore it, warnings like this are often wrong | Drive towards the storm to film it
exercise: A flood warning is given and water is rising in the street. What is safest? | Move up to a higher floor and keep out of the water | Walk through the water to a friend's house where it is drier | Play in the water while it is shallow | Go down to the basement
exercise: Why should a cut hand be raised after you press a cloth on it? | It helps to slow the bleeding | It keeps the cloth nice and dry | It makes the cut close at once | It stops the hand from shaking
exercise: Why is the five-second rule wrong? | Germs on the floor stick to food the moment it lands | Germs only live on dirty floors | Germs need five whole seconds to wake up and climb onto the food | Germs cannot climb onto dry food
exercise: In a flood, why should you go upstairs rather than down? | Water fills the lowest places first | Upstairs rooms are always much warmer | Floodwater cannot climb stairs at all | Basements always stay dry in a flood
test: Why is it dangerous to walk through even shallow floodwater? | It can be fast, dirty and carry electricity from fallen wires | It is always warm and makes you sleepy | Floodwater is only dangerous at night | It is just clean rainwater from the sky, so it is safe to walk through
test: A family in New South Wales hears a bushfire warning on the radio. Which plan is best? | Do what their emergency plan says and follow official alerts | Wait until they can see the flames themselves | Go towards the fire to help | Turn the radio off to stay calm
test: A boy in Canada drops a sandwich and says «five-second rule!». What is true? | Germs got on it at once; the rule is a myth | It is safe because he was quick | It is safe on carpet but not on tiles, because carpet is soft | It is safe if nobody saw it fall
test: Why does a closed bedroom door help in a house fire? | It slows the smoke and flames from coming in | It makes the fire go out by itself | It makes the room hotter so all the smoke rises up and away | Fire cannot burn painted doors
test: Why is a dirty splinter more of a problem than a clean one? | Germs on it can cause an infection | Dirty splinters are always much bigger | Clean splinters come out by themselves | Dirt makes the splinter go in deeper
test: Why does pressing a cloth on a cut help? | The pressure helps the bleeding stop | The cloth soaks up the pain | It keeps the finger warm | It makes the cut close up and heal over in just a second
test: Why is grit or salt put on icy steps? | It stops people slipping | It makes the steps look clean | It makes the ice even thicker | It keeps snow from falling
test: A girl wants to fix a broken hairdryer. What must happen before anyone opens it? | It is unplugged, because a switch can fail | It is switched off at the button and left plugged in | It is cooled in the freezer | It is shaken to see if it works
test: Your cousin dares you to drink a little washing-up liquid. What do you say? | No, cleaning products can burn your throat | Yes, a little is harmless | Only if you add sugar | Only if you drink water first
test: dropped my biscuit, 5 sec rule!! | No, germs get on it at once; if in doubt, bin it | Yes, under five seconds it's clean | Yes, if you blow on it | Only the floor of a shop is dirty, home floors are clean enough
test: cut my finger peeling apples, bleedin a lot | Press a clean cloth on it and hold your hand up | Hold it under hot water for ages | Let it bleed to clean it | Put it in your mouth to clean it and keep peeling the apples
test: flood outside, can we walk thru it its only shallow | No, floodwater can be fast, dirty and electrified; go higher | Yes, if you wear wellies | Yes, if it's below your knees and you walk slowly in the middle | Yes, if you hold hands
test: toaster's switched off at the button, can i poke the bread out | Unplug it first, a switch can fail | Yes, the switch makes it safe | Yes, with a metal knife quickly | Yes, if the light is off
explain: A boy in Leeds believed the myth that you should put butter on a burn; he did, and the burn blistered badly until he cooled it with water at the doctor's.
explain: A girl in Ohio ignored a "wet floor" sign at the shopping centre; she slipped and broke her wrist.
fact: Warning signs and labels are there because people have been hurt in that exact way before.
fact: Common home first-aid myths, such as butter on burns or tipping the head back for nosebleeds, can make injuries worse; a burn needs cool running water for twenty minutes.
fact: If someone faints, get an adult, lay them down and raise their legs a little.
test: sign says wet floor but i'm in a rush | Walk round it or go slow, you could slip and break a bone | Run across fast | Ignore it, those signs are just there for grown-ups and cleaners | Skate across it
test: grandad says put toothpaste on a burn | No, cool running water for 20 minutes is right | Yes, toothpaste cools the burn and is the best thing to use | Yes, with butter too | Use ice for an hour
test: mate fainted at school | Get an adult, lay him down and lift his legs a little | Throw a cup of cold water on his face to wake him up quickly | Shake him hard | Make him stand up fast
test: my nan swears butter on a burn fixes it, she's done it "100 times". right? | No, cool the burn under running water instead | Yes, butter seals the burn and stops the pain | Yes, but only use salted butter on it | Yes, butter then a plaster on top of it
test: did u hear a kid walked thru floodwater n "a shark bit him on his street". what's probs a rumour | The shark; but floodwater really is fast, dirty and electrified | That floodwater can be electrified | That you should not walk in floodwater | That in a flood you should move up to the higher floors of the house
test: kids say Zoe slipped on a wet floor at the mall cos she "can't read signs lol". pass it on? | No, it is unkind; the lesson is to heed warning signs | Yes, it is funny and everyone at school should get to hear it | Yes, but only to kids in her class | Yes, as it might teach her a lesson
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called five-second rule concerning dropped food? | It is untrue, as germs stick to the food at once | It is true, as germs need five seconds to climb | It is true for all foods dropped upon carpet | It is true provided that the food is dry
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an appliance is unplugged before anyone touches its interior. | A switch may fail and leave it live | Unplugging prevents the appliance from cooling | The plug may otherwise overheat and melt | It makes the appliance easier to lift
exam: It is given that a flood is occurring. Determine the conduct prescribed for the members of a household. | Move to higher floors and never walk in floodwater | Wade outside at once to reach the nearest shop | Stay in the cellar where it is coldest and dry | Drive the car through the water to escape it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning first aid for a bleeding cut. | Press a clean cloth on it and raise the hand | Hold it under hot water until it stops at once | Rub some butter on it and leave it uncovered | Squeeze it firmly to push the dirt out of it
